# GENERATED by `KORVUS_UPDATE_BRIDGE_SLOTS=1 cargo test -p korvus-runtime --test all -- bridge_slots::`
# -- every `KB.__*` slot that exists in a RUNNING realm (SSOT W12.5), read off the holder itself.
#
# Invariant I3 says a member's body is a Rust callback or the D2 stub, with no `KB.__*` bridge. The
# bodies half has held for a while. The bridge half was FALSE and nothing in the tree could see it:
# `scripts/freeze.sh`'s `kb_slots` ratchet greps `KB.__x =` spellings out of `.js` files and the
# kernel's Rust STRING LITERALS, so `bridge_set(scope, "__x", …)` called from Rust read as nothing at
# all. The ratchet sat at 0, honestly, while four slots lived under it for months.
#
# SO THIS IS NOT A GREP. `core/korvus-runtime/tests/bridge_slots.rs` boots each realm, reaches the
# holder at `globalThis[Symbol.for("korvus.bridge")]` and reads its OWN KEYS, then compares that list
# to the rows below. Source can be spelled around -- a name built at runtime, a helper one call deeper,
# a slot written from a crate the ratchet does not scan. The object's own key list cannot: whatever
# route put a slot there, it is there, and this fails.
#
# It fails in BOTH directions. A slot in a realm with no row fails. A row whose slot a realm no longer
# has fails too -- delete the mount and the row goes in the SAME commit. `scripts/freeze.sh`'s
# `bridge_slots_live` ratchet counts these rows, so the number only ever falls.
#
# columns, tab-separated:  <realm>  <slot>  <what mounts it>  <why it is allowed to exist>
#
# `realm` is one of: window, worker (a DedicatedWorker), shared (a SharedWorker), service (a
# ServiceWorker). NO REALM HAS ROWS AND NONE MAY GAIN ONE: none has a holder, so `Symbol(korvus.bridge)`
# is absent from every global rather than filtered out of it -- the window's since SSOT W12.5, the three
# workers' since TEMPLATES P7 (B28), when their last two slots' callers became Rust.
